    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- IMPACT -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        id="impact"
        eyebrow="Impact"
        title="From hours of investigation to a shared source of truth"
      />
      <p class="body-copy" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
        <em>Awaiting <code>ImpactCard</code> / <code>MetricRow</code> / <code>Quote</code> — content:</em>
      </p>
      <ul class="body-copy" style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li>
          <strong>Recover change context</strong> — AFTER ✅ ~5 min · BEFORE up to 1 working day. Quote: "Designers can now retrieve the context behind a past component change without reconstructing it from Slack, email and conversations"
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Release note: everyone up-to-date</strong> — AFTER ✅ ~25 min of review · BEFORE ~4h manual work. Quote: "The weekly release note is generated automatically from the same structured change data and sent to Slack for review."
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reduce the risk of outdated components</strong> — AFTER ✅ Component history and replacement context are available where needed · BEFORE Changes could be missed and outdated versions could reach final files
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- LEARNINGS -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        id="learnings"
        eyebrow="Learnings"
        title="What this project changed in the way I work"
      />
      <p class="body-copy" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
        <em>Awaiting <code>LearningRow</code> — content:</em>
      </p>
      <div class="body-copy">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">
          <strong>I learned how to do design in a "terminal"</strong>
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;">
          The faster I could generate and change working software, the easier it became to lose the rationale behind decisions. I kept a lightweight change and decision log and repeatedly checked new ideas against the original goals to avoid scope drift.
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">
          <strong>I learned to never trust AI, no matter how confident it sounds</strong>
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          AI suggestions can sound convincing, especially outside your strongest technical domain. I learned to challenge them, ask for alternatives and validate doubts with experts. One example was moving from one large Markdown changelog to a component-centric structure after investigating retrieval and maintainability.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
